---
import EditorialFrame from '../../components/EditorialFrame.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import { services, fmtDate } from '../../lib/data.js';
import { newestDate } from '../../lib/editorial.js';

const svcs = [...services.CA.values()];
const perCountry = svcs.filter(s => s.hasApplicantPages);
const updated = svcs.map(s => s.latestEffective).sort().at(-1);
const citations = [...new Set(svcs.flatMap(service => service.records.map(record => record.source_url)))];
---
<EditorialFrame sectionName="Guides" sectionPath="/guides/" slug="how-canada-processing-times-work"
  title="How Canada IRCC processing times work — current guide"
  description="Learn how IRCC calculates processing times, why figures change, what missing data means, and how to use current medians without treating them as promises."
  h1="How Canada's processing times actually work"
  lede={`Immigration, Refugees and Citizenship Canada publishes, for most temporary-residence routes, a processing time per applicant country. Here is how to read those figures, built on our tracked copy of the data (last official update ${fmtDate(updated)}).`}
  published="2026-08-22" modified={newestDate('2026-08-23', updated)} checked={updated}
  about={['IRCC processing times', 'Canada immigration']} citations={citations} adPrefix="guide-ca-method">
  <AdSlot position="guide-top" />

  <div class="callout warn"><strong>IRCC publishes two different kinds of time.</strong><p>Country-by-country temporary-residence figures are generally backward-looking. Selected permanent-residence and citizenship programs use monthly forward-looking projections. GovWait labels them separately because comparing the two as if they used one method would be misleading.</p></div>

  <h2>Country-by-country figures are a rear-view mirror</h2>
  <p>IRCC's published figure answers a precise question: <em>how long did it recently take to finalize most applications of this type from this country?</em> It is recalculated from completed cases (typically the time in which ~80% of recent applications were processed). That has three practical consequences:</p>
  <p><strong>1. It moves constantly.</strong> A surge of applications, a processing blitz, or a backlog clean-up shifts the statistic within weeks. That is why we record every published value instead of quoting one — the direction of movement is often more informative than the level.</p>
  <p><strong>2. It is not a promise.</strong> Your case can be faster or much slower than the figure, depending on documentation completeness, security screening, and volumes. Treat it as the best available base rate, not a deadline.</p>
  <p><strong>3. Fast can mean empty.</strong> A very short published time from a small country sometimes reflects a tiny sample of easy cases rather than generous capacity.</p>

  <h2>Forward-looking programs estimate the road ahead</h2>
  <p>For selected permanent-residence and citizenship programs, IRCC estimates how long a complete application received today may take. It uses the number of applications already waiting, expected processing capacity, expected new applications and—in some programs—annual admissions levels. These figures update monthly and carry <code>metric_type: "forward"</code> in the GovWait API.</p>
  <p>If someone already applied, IRCC also publishes a current estimate for the month they applied and an approximate number of people ahead. Those application-month rows are one current snapshot, not old publication history. See the <a href="/canada/canadian-experience-class/">Canadian Experience Class projection and cohort table</a> for a live example.</p>

  <h2>Current medians per service</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>Countries with a published time</th><th>Median</th><th>Last updated</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {perCountry.map(s => (
        <tr>
          <td><a href={`/canada/${s.slug}/`}>{s.name}</a></td>
          <td class="num">{s.okCount} of {s.records.length}</td>
          <td class="num">{s.medianDays ? `${Math.round(s.medianDays)} days` : '—'}</td>
          <td class="num">{fmtDate(s.latestEffective)}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>What “no processing time available” means</h2>
  <p>For many country–service pairs, IRCC publishes no figure at all. This is usually a volume threshold: too few recent applications from that country to compute a stable statistic. It says nothing about your eligibility, and applications from those countries are processed normally. We list those routes anyway, dated — because the moment a figure appears, that appearance is itself news about the corridor.</p>

  <h2>How to use these pages well</h2>
  <p>Check your exact route (service + your country), note the official update date next to the value, glance at the recorded history for direction, and re-check before you book anything expensive. Every page links the official IRCC source — for decisions that matter, confirm there. Nothing here is legal advice.</p>

  <div class="prov">All figures on this page are computed from the tracked dataset at build time — they update automatically with every refresh.</div>
  <AdSlot position="guide-bottom" />
</EditorialFrame>
